<section id="access">
  <h2>What Distilled can and cannot do</h2>
  <div class="docs-rows">
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Can</strong>
      <p>Read pull request metadata, deployment statuses, published releases, environment names and repository metadata, for the repositories you explicitly granted access to and nothing else.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Cannot</strong>
      <p>Write anything. No permission Distilled holds allows creating, editing, merging, closing or commenting on anything in GitHub. Every scope it requests is read-only, and there is no code path that writes to GitHub.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The complete permission list, and the reason for each, is on <a href="/docs/github-app">The GitHub App</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="stored">
  <h2>What's stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pull request facts</strong>: number, title, author login, target branch, draft state, and the opened, merged and closed timestamps.</li>
    <li><strong>Deployment facts</strong>: when a production deployment succeeded or a release was published, and which repository it belonged to.</li>
    <li><strong>Repository and environment metadata</strong>: names, default branch, and whether an environment is treated as production.</li>
    <li><strong>Computed metrics</strong>: the daily and weekly aggregates behind the charts.</li>
    <li><strong>Account information</strong>: your name, email and profile from GitHub, held by our authentication provider.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What isn't</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your source code.</strong> Not cloned, not read, not stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffs, file contents or commit messages.</strong> None of them are needed to compute the metrics.</li>
    <li><strong>Raw webhook payloads.</strong> Events are parsed into the facts above and the payload is discarded. Only delivery metadata (an id, a type, a size) is kept, so we can tell whether an event was processed.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="isolation">
  <h2>Isolation between workspaces</h2>
  <p>Every record Distilled stores is tagged with the workspace it belongs to, and every request verifies your membership of the workspace it's asking about on the server, not in the browser.</p>
</section>

<section id="integrity">
  <h2>How events are trusted</h2>
  <p>Every incoming webhook is verified with an HMAC signature before it's parsed, so nothing can inject delivery data by posting to the endpoint. A delivery with a missing or wrong signature is rejected without being processed. Repeated deliveries of the same event are recorded once.</p>
  <p>Binding a GitHub installation to a workspace requires proof that you control that installation, so you can't attach someone else's organisation to your workspace by guessing its id.</p>
</section>

<section id="removing">
  <h2>Removing your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stop new data:</strong> uninstall the Distilled app from GitHub, or narrow the repositories it can see. Event delivery stops immediately.</li>
    <li><strong>Hide a repository:</strong> remove it in Settings → Repositories. Its history is retained but hidden, and adding it back restores it.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete everything:</strong> a sole owner can delete a workspace from Settings → Team. That removes the workspace and all of its repositories, pull requests, deployments and metrics permanently.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For any request beyond that, email <a href="mailto:hello@distilledmetrics.com">hello@distilledmetrics.com</a>. Retention and your rights under data protection law are set out in the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="open-source">
  <h2>Verify it yourself</h2>
  <p>Distilled is open source under the AGPL-3.0 licence. The webhook handlers, the metric queries and the permission set are all in the <a href="https://github.com/mattpauldavies/distilled">public repository</a>, so you don't have to take any of this on trust. See <a href="/docs/self-hosting">Self-hosting</a> if you'd rather run it yourself.</p>
  <p>Found a security issue? Email <a href="mailto:hello@distilledmetrics.com">hello@distilledmetrics.com</a> rather than opening a public issue.</p>
</section>

